48
00:03:29,195 --> 00:03:33,785
The oral part needs a second person or
the tutor. If you rehearse alone, record

49
00:03:33,825 --> 00:03:36,480
yourself and score the
recording the next day.

50
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:41,507
Why are the questions shaped this way?
Because the goal of the course is diagnosis

51
00:03:41,547 --> 00:03:44,792
from first principles. A command
recited is worth little.

52
00:03:44,832 --> 00:03:49,346
A mechanism, stated with the file, the
reader and the failure, is what lets you

53
00:03:49,386 --> 00:03:51,364
answer a question you haven't seen.

54
00:03:53,204 --> 00:03:57,328
Prepare with one sentence pattern,
applied to every structure of this part:

55
00:03:57,668 --> 00:04:01,649
"It stores this. This reads it.
If it is stale, this happens.

56
00:04:01,689 --> 00:04:03,758
If it is missing, this happens."

57
00:04:04,098 --> 00:04:07,544
Try it on the pack index, the
lookup file beside a pack.

58
00:04:07,584 --> 00:04:12,274
It stores object IDs with offsets into
a pack. Every object lookup reads it.

59
00:04:12,314 --> 00:04:17,378
It can't be stale, since a pack never
changes. If it's missing, Git warns, and you

60
00:04:17,418 --> 00:04:20,615
rebuild it from the pack,
because it's derived data.

61
00:04:20,955 --> 00:04:26,175
Try it on the commit-graph. It stores
parents, generation numbers and changed-path

62
00:04:26,215 --> 00:04:29,574
filters. History walks and
path-limited logs read it.

63
00:04:29,614 --> 00:04:33,231
Stale: slower, not wrong.
Missing: Git reads the objects.

64
00:04:33,571 --> 00:04:38,789
If you can do that for objects, packs, refs,
the index and each cache, you're ready

65
00:04:38,829 --> 00:04:44,111
for Parts 1 and 4. The pattern breaks only
where data is primary: for an object or a

66
00:04:44,151 --> 00:04:47,405
reflog, "missing" has no
rebuild, only another copy.

67
00:04:47,745 --> 00:04:54,018
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Write
five words: objects, packs, refs, index,

68
00:04:54,058 --> 00:04:58,226
caches. Under each, write
primary or derived. I'll wait.

69
00:05:04,566 --> 00:05:10,418
Five boxes: objects, packs, refs, index,
caches. Under each, the commands that read

70
00:05:10,458 --> 00:05:15,954
it in every format. Nowhere on this slide
is there a cat of a file under .git.

71
00:05:16,294 --> 00:05:19,613
Now check your five answers
against the bottom line.

72
00:05:19,653 --> 00:05:24,908
It's the distinction from the first page
of Chapter 3: before you delete or repair

73
00:05:24,948 --> 00:05:29,106
anything under .git, decide whether
it's primary or derived data.

74
00:05:29,146 --> 00:05:33,562
An index or a pack index can be
rebuilt. An object or a reflog can't.

75
00:05:37,902 --> 00:05:43,410
Six commits, one file of 200 lines changed
five times, one small configuration file.

76
00:05:43,450 --> 00:05:47,108
Say the count before it appears,
and say why. I'll wait.

77
00:05:51,948 --> 00:05:53,503
Twenty-five, all loose.

78
00:05:53,843 --> 00:05:58,658
State what git gc changes in .git, in
one sentence, with the file names.

79
00:05:58,698 --> 00:05:59,737
Say it out loud.

80
00:06:04,577 --> 00:06:07,848
Nothing loose now, and
one pack holds all 25.

81
00:06:08,188 --> 00:06:13,157
Quick quiz, two options. Which version of
the file is stored whole, the newest or

82
00:06:13,197 --> 00:06:16,991
the oldest? And how will the
listing show it? Say it out loud.

83
00:06:29,831 --> 00:06:35,514
The newest, a4e58256. Each older version's
line adds a chain depth and the ID of its

84
00:06:35,554 --> 00:06:35,854
base.

85
00:06:36,194 --> 00:06:39,915
Last one. The oldest version
costs 35 bytes in the pack.

86
00:06:39,955 --> 00:06:45,221
What does git cat-file -p return for it,
and what does that tell you about "Git

87
00:06:45,261 --> 00:06:47,907
stores snapshots"? Make your prediction.

88
00:06:59,547 --> 00:07:04,728
All 200 lines. The pack saves space, and
the object you read back is still whole.

89
00:07:05,068 --> 00:07:10,248
If you could narrate those four snippets
without the book, with the columns named,

90
00:07:10,288 --> 00:07:15,533
