92
00:07:17,019 --> 00:07:21,722
For the hands-on part, four commands
are your instruments: git cat-file

93
00:07:21,762 --> 00:07:27,334
--batch-all-objects --batch-check to see
every object, git for-each-ref to see every

94
00:07:27,374 --> 00:07:32,879
ref, git count-objects -v for storage, and
git fsck for connectivity and integrity.

95
00:07:32,919 --> 00:07:36,620
They work whether refs are
loose, packed or in reftable.

96
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:39,493
Those four replace the
habit from the hook.

97
00:07:41,333 --> 00:07:42,981
Five mistakes to watch for.

98
00:07:43,321 --> 00:07:47,377
Reading .git/refs or packed-refs
by hand in the hands-on part.

99
00:07:47,417 --> 00:07:52,728
Root cause: a ref can be in one of several
storage locations; only plumbing gives

100
00:07:52,768 --> 00:07:54,974
the current value in every format.

101
00:07:55,314 --> 00:08:00,279
Running a prune or git gc --prune=now
to "clean up" a dangling line.

102
00:08:00,319 --> 00:08:06,019
Root cause: dangling describes fsck's
starting points; the prune destroys what

103
00:08:06,059 --> 00:08:07,123
recovery needs.

104
00:08:07,463 --> 00:08:11,739
Answering a concept question with a
command. Root cause: the gate asks for a

105
00:08:11,779 --> 00:08:14,635
mechanism: which file, which
reader, which failure.

106
00:08:14,975 --> 00:08:20,364
Passing the total and missing a part. Root
cause: the pass rule also requires 70% in

107
00:08:20,404 --> 00:08:21,851
each of the four parts.

108
00:08:22,191 --> 00:08:27,415
Opening generate.sh or check.sh first.
Root cause: they are the answer to "what

109
00:08:27,455 --> 00:08:30,147
happened" and the list of end conditions.

110
00:08:31,987 --> 00:08:36,213
Now, out of the lab. Think of the
gate as a rehearsal of a real hour.

111
00:08:36,253 --> 00:08:41,222
A build server's repository misbehaves.
The report from the person who noticed is

112
00:08:41,262 --> 00:08:46,354
